from authoring import finish,ref,step,paragraph
finish('regular-language-generalized-star-height-greater-than-one',
 id='TCS-6559',title='Does any regular language require generalized star height greater than one?',area='Automata and formal languages',criterion='characterization',question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Does there exist a finite nonempty alphabet Σ and a regular language L⊆Σ* such that every generalized regular expression denoting L has Kleene-star nesting depth at least two, when complement is allowed without increasing that depth?',
 definitions='Σ* is the set of all finite words over Σ, including the empty word ε. A regular language is one accepted by a finite deterministic automaton. Generalized expressions are finite expressions built from ∅, ε, and the letters a∈Σ using binary union E∪F, concatenation EF, complement E^c, and Kleene star E*. Their meanings are respectively the empty language, {ε}, {a}, language union, all concatenations uv with u∈L(E) and v∈L(F), Σ*∖L(E), and the union of all finite concatenation powers of L(E), including power zero {ε}. Set h(∅)=h(ε)=h(a)=0, h(E∪F)=h(EF)=max{h(E),h(F)}, h(E^c)=h(E), and h(E*)=h(E)+1. Define h_g(L)=min{h(E):L(E)=L}, which exists because every regular language has an ordinary regular expression. The question is whether h_g(L)>1 for at least one such L. There is no bound on expression size, number of complements, union or concatenation depth, automaton size, or alphabet size. Intersection is permitted as the abbreviation (E^c∪F^c)^c and does not add star depth. Backreferences, infinite words, additional predicates and auxiliary alphabets in the final expression are not part of the syntax.',
 answer_criterion='Give a finite automaton or another explicit regular description of one language L and prove h_g(L)≥2, or prove that every regular language over every finite alphabet has an equivalent generalized expression of star height at most one. A nested expression for L does not prove a lower bound: all equivalent expressions must be excluded. An example of large ordinary star height without complement, a lower bound assuming a size limit, or an algorithm that fails to find a shallow expression does not settle this question.',
 context=[
 paragraph('Kleene star represents arbitrary finite repetition. Nested stars arise naturally when repetitions occur inside other repeated blocks. The question is whether such nesting expresses a real hierarchy once a language can also be complemented at no cost to the height measure. Complement preserves regularity, so it does not enlarge the class recognized by finite automata. It may nevertheless radically change the shape of the shortest-depth expression for the same language.'),
 paragraph('Even elementary examples demonstrate that syntax and language height must be separated. Over Σ={a,b}, the language of all words has the ordinary expression (a∪b)*, with a star. With complement, it is simply ∅^c and has height zero. The language avoiding the factor ab is (Σ*abΣ*)^c; replacing each displayed Σ* by ∅^c makes this a star-free expression. Infinitely many words can therefore belong to a language of generalized height zero.'),
 paragraph('Height zero does not capture all regular languages. Over the unary alphabet {a}, the even-length language (aa)* has generalized height exactly one. Its two-state automaton remembers length modulo two. Schützenberger’s theorem characterizes height zero through an aperiodic syntactic monoid, and this parity language has a nontrivial two-element group. Thus the first separation, between zero and one, is known; the question asks for the next separation.','survey'),
 paragraph('The syntactic monoid packages how words behave inside arbitrary contexts: u and v are equivalent when puq belongs to L exactly when pvq does, for every pair of words p,q. Concatenation induces multiplication on the finitely many equivalence classes. A finite monoid is aperiodic when some exponent N makes x^N=x^{N+1} for every element x. Schützenberger’s characterization gives an effective algebraic test for star-freeness, but no corresponding complete height-one criterion is known.','pst'),
 paragraph('The ordinary, or restricted, star-height problem disallows complement. In that setting, arbitrarily high heights exist and the minimum height is computable. These facts cannot be transferred to generalized height by reusing an ordinary expression or an automaton cycle measure: allowing complement introduces additional descriptions that the old lower-bound argument may not exclude. Pin’s problem page explicitly distinguishes these two versions and separately lists the existence of height two and the algorithmic computation of height.','survey'),
 paragraph('Pin, Straubing and Thérien show that several apparently complicated algebraic classes still yield height at most one, including languages recognized by finite nilpotent groups of class at most two. They also eliminate a previously proposed height-two candidate. Their inverse-morphism result gives a useful warning: every regular language is the inverse image of an ordinary height-one language under some morphism. Proving that generalized height one is closed under all inverse morphisms would therefore force a universal collapse; that closure cannot be silently assumed.','pst'),
 paragraph('Bourne and Ruškuc extend the height-one territory using factor-counting arguments. Their results cover specified counting cases and languages recognized by Rees zero-matrix semigroups over abelian groups. A factor here is a contiguous occurrence of a word, which differs from counting scattered subsequences. The theorem handles a substantial recognizing-semigroup class, while leaving the existence of a language above height one unresolved.','rees'),
 paragraph('Daviaud and Paperman study languages generated from sets u* with a single word u, under Boolean or lattice operations and, in some cases, quotients. They give effective equational characterizations of those particular classes. These are deliberately restricted families inside the broader problem: a full height-one expression may take the star of an arbitrary star-free language and combine the resulting languages by concatenation and Boolean operations. Their paper describes its result as a step toward that larger structural challenge.','wordstar'),
 paragraph('Cotumaccio’s 2025 account of connections between compressed indexing and automata again identifies generalized star height as an open structural question. It does not supply a new separation or universal flattening theorem. The relevance extends beyond a formal-expression puzzle: a solution would clarify how finite-state iteration, modular behaviour and Boolean operations interact, while allowing descriptions to grow arbitrarily large.','compression')],
 why='A basic unresolved expressive-depth question for regular languages: determine whether Boolean operations eliminate every need for nested iteration, or whether a genuine second level exists.',
 importance=dict(score=96,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-11',reason='A longstanding foundational problem connecting regular expressions, finite automata, algebraic language theory and logical descriptions.'),
 progress=[
 step('1965','Schützenberger characterizes generalized height zero by aperiodic syntactic monoids; height one is already necessary for simple parity languages.','survey'),
 step('1992','Pin–Straubing–Thérien prove additional height-one classes and remove a proposed height-two candidate.','pst'),
 step('2016-09-27','Bourne–Ruškuc’s revised manuscript establishes height-one bounds for specified factor-counting languages and Rees zero-matrix semigroups over abelian groups.','rees'),
 step('2018','Daviaud–Paperman give effective characterizations of restricted classes generated by stars of individual words.','wordstar'),
 step('2025','Cotumaccio’s automata/compression survey still identifies generalized star height among the open structural questions.','compression')],
 references=[
 ref('survey','The star-height problem','Jean-Éric Pin',2013,'https://www.irif.fr/~jep/Problemes/starheight.html','Author problem page, last modified 8 August 2013; definitions and distinct generalized versus restricted questions'),
 ref('pst','Some results on the generalized star-height problem','Jean-Éric Pin; Howard Straubing; Denis Thérien',1992,'https://hal.science/hal-00019978','Information and Computation 101(2), 219–250; author PDF dated May 2002 and HAL deposit March 2006 are later versions/hosting dates'),
 ref('rees','On the star-height of factor counting languages and their relationship to Rees zero-matrix semigroups','Tom Bourne; Nik Ruškuc',2016,'https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.06236','v2, 27 September; §§1–3; PDF title uses subword counting, explicitly meaning contiguous factors'),
 ref('wordstar','Classes of languages generated by the Kleene star of a word','Laure Daviaud; Charles Paperman',2018,'https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/id/eprint/104953/','Information and Computation 262, 90–109; introduction and effective equational characterizations; MFCS 2015 precursor'),
 ref('compression','Data Compression Meets Automata Theory','Nicola Cotumaccio',2025,'https://bulletin.eatcs.org/index.php/beatcs/article/view/824','Bulletin of the EATCS 145, Algorithmics Column; closing discussion links automata characterizations to the open generalized star-height problem')],
 status='source_open',status_note='No separating language or universal height-one theorem was found in the primary literature checked through 11 September 2026. The most recent primary discussion located here is the 2025 EATCS column; the 2016 and 2018 results concern proper scoped families. This card fixes the existence-of-height-greater-than-one question rather than the separate general decidability question.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-11')
